1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,120
Welcome, corruption is not forgotten – this is exactly the title and the fascinating question

2
00:00:06,120 --> 00:00:07,919
at the heart of today's analysis.

3
00:00:07,919 --> 00:01:12,919
A true journey through history, economic and political psychology, we dive into a study

4
00:00:12,919 --> 00:00:18,400
scientific, highly detailed, to understand how a single traumatic institutional event

5
00:00:18,400 --> 00:00:23,559
can permanently alter the electoral behavior of an entire generation.

6
00:00:23,559 --> 00:00:24,560
Let's begin.

7
00:00:24,560 --> 00:00:28,240
There is a reason for the lack of trust in government.

8
00:00:28,240 --> 00:00:29,400
Corruption.

9
00:00:29,400 --> 00:00:34,400
Unequivocal words spoken in 2018 by Christine Lagarde, President of the European Central Bank,

10
00:00:34,400 --> 00:00:38,920
and they throw us right into the heart of the modern enigma we are experiencing.

11
00:00:38,920 --> 00:00:42,760
Across the Western world, trust in institutions plummets while populist movements

12
00:00:42,760 --> 00:00:44,240
gain ground.

13
00:00:44,240 --> 00:00:49,040
Well, economic research suggests that the perception of systemic corruption is

14
00:00:49,040 --> 00:00:53,520
the real turning point, capable of triggering chain reactions that last for decades.

15
00:00:53,520 --> 00:00:58,400
To fully understand this mechanism, we need to take a leap back in time, specifically

16
00:00:58,399 --> 00:01:03,399
to Italy in the early 90s, a period that can only be described as a political earthquake.

17
00:01:03,399 --> 00:01:09,599
In 1992, the Maxi-investigation "Mani Pulite" began, a shock of epic proportions; consider

18
00:01:09,599 --> 00:01:15,400
that a full 23% of Italian parliamentarians ended up accused of corruption, a total collapse

19
00:01:15,400 --> 00:01:21,400
of the system, which directly led to the unprecedented national elections of 1994.

20
00:01:21,400 --> 00:01:23,599
And the media impact?

21
00:01:23,599 --> 00:01:25,400
Something never seen before.

22
00:01:25,400 --> 00:01:29,400
Data extracted from the study shows an absolute explosion.

23
00:01:29,400 --> 00:01:36,840
Between 1992 and 1994, mentions of the word "corruption" in major newspapers like

24
00:01:36,840 --> 00:01:42,400
Corriere della Sera and minutes dedicated in TV news reached historic peaks.

25
00:01:42,400 --> 00:01:47,280
The incredible thing is that the topic of corruption even surpassed football, which in Italy

26
00:01:47,280 --> 00:01:49,400
has always been the most discussed topic.

27
00:01:49,400 --> 00:01:54,400
In short, total media saturation for anyone living in those years.

28
00:01:54,400 --> 00:01:59,400
Now, looking through the scientific lens of this study, a key concept comes into play:

29
00:01:59,400 --> 00:02:01,400
the impressionable years hypothesis.

30
00:02:01,400 --> 00:02:05,900
This is a very fascinating psychological theory, which states that young adults

31
00:02:05,900 --> 00:02:10,400
are affected much more deeply by major social shocks than older people.

32
00:02:10,400 --> 00:02:11,400
older people.

33
00:02:11,400 --> 00:02:16,099
The reason is very simple: their worldview, their beliefs, are still

34
00:02:16,099 --> 00:02:17,099
forming.

35
00:02:17,099 --> 00:02:21,400
And once molded in that phase, they tend to crystallize for the rest of their lives.

36
00:02:21,400 --> 00:02:23,400
It's like clay that hardens.

37
00:02:23,900 --> 00:02:27,400
Immediately, another fundamental hypothesis joins this one.

38
00:02:27,400 --> 00:02:30,400
The first-time voter hypothesis.

39
00:02:30,400 --> 00:02:35,599
In political science, it is established that 18 and 19-year-olds, when called to the polls

40
00:02:35,599 --> 00:02:39,400
for the very first time, experience an enormous informational shock.

41
00:02:39,400 --> 00:02:42,400
The very act of voting is an absolute novelty.

42
00:02:42,400 --> 00:02:46,900
Therefore, their level of attention towards electoral campaigns and political news

43
00:02:46,900 --> 00:02:48,900
is simply at an all-time high.

44
00:02:49,400 --> 00:02:54,400
And so, research crosses these two theories to surgically identify

45
00:02:54,400 --> 00:02:56,400
a very specific group.

46
00:02:56,400 --> 00:03:01,400
Italians born in 1975 and 1976.

47
00:03:01,400 --> 00:03:06,900
This is the generation, the reference cohort, at the center of the entire study.

48
00:03:06,900 --> 00:03:11,640
They are precisely the young people who cast their very first ballot in the heated

49
00:03:11,640 --> 00:03:13,900
1994 elections.

50
00:03:13,900 --> 00:03:19,900
Practically a perfect storm: a very young generation, psychologically very malleable,

51
00:03:19,900 --> 00:03:24,599
exposed to the greatest institutional crisis in republican history, precisely on the day

52
00:03:24,599 --> 00:03:26,599
of its democratic debut.

53
00:03:26,599 --> 00:03:29,800
But the question is, did this trauma then fade away over time?

54
00:03:29,800 --> 00:03:36,599
Researchers analyzed the Oxer data collected 25 years later, in 2017, to find out.

55
00:03:36,599 --> 00:03:38,800
And the answer is a resounding no.

56
00:03:38,800 --> 00:03:43,300
Three pillars of the state suffered a true and permanent collapse of legitimacy

57
00:03:43,300 --> 00:03:45,300
for this specific generation.

58
00:03:45,300 --> 00:03:50,300
We're talking about the National Parliament, the National Government, and public employees.

59
00:03:50,300 --> 00:03:53,300
A wound that never healed.

60
00:03:53,300 --> 00:03:57,600
The true turning point of the study emerges by comparing these very voters,

61
00:03:57,600 --> 00:04:01,300
the debutantes of 1994, with all other generations.

62
00:04:01,300 --> 00:04:06,800
Statistical data clinically describes a chronic negative shift in trust

63
00:04:06,800 --> 00:04:09,800
in institutions for this specific treated group.

64
00:04:09,800 --> 00:04:13,300
Note, this is not the classic disillusionment that comes with age.

65
00:04:13,300 --> 00:04:18,100
No, it's an anomalous trust deficit found only in those who faced the baptism

66
00:04:18,100 --> 00:04:21,300
of voting, crushed by the weight of that epochal scandal.

67
00:04:21,300 --> 00:04:24,300
The numbers confirm everything in an impressive way.

68
00:04:24,300 --> 00:04:30,500
A drop in trust of between 14% and 29% less, measured in standard deviations, was recorded

69
00:04:30,500 --> 00:04:32,500
for those specific institutions.

70
00:04:32,500 --> 00:04:38,300
A truly massive statistical decline, and it's fundamental to reiterate the rigor of the scientific method applied.

71
00:04:38,300 --> 00:04:42,500
Researchers isolated and controlled every possible variable, from education to

72
00:04:42,500 --> 00:04:44,800
region, gender, and even religion.

73
00:04:44,800 --> 00:04:49,300
These are solid empirical evidences of a distrust that remains intact a quarter

74
00:04:49,300 --> 00:04:50,800
of a century later.

75
00:04:50,800 --> 00:04:57,300
The study, however, doesn't stop at mere opinions or surveys; it takes a further, decisive leap,

76
00:04:57,300 --> 00:05:02,500
asking how this chronic lack of faith translates into the secret of the ballot box,

77
00:05:02,500 --> 00:05:03,500
decades later.

78
00:05:03,500 --> 00:05:09,500
Well, a tangible, persistent shift towards populist voting was observed during

79
00:05:09,500 --> 00:05:11,899
the national elections of 2018.

80
00:05:11,899 --> 00:05:17,300
The cynicism towards the establishment and moderate parties, born and incubated in the 90s, found

81
00:05:17,300 --> 00:05:21,500
years later a powerful and concrete electoral outlet.

82
00:05:21,500 --> 00:05:26,500
More precisely, there is an eleven percent higher probability that this

83
00:05:26,500 --> 00:05:31,500
specific generation voted for anti-system platforms in 2018.

84
00:05:31,500 --> 00:05:36,500
Looking at the data with absolute and rigorous political neutrality, what is scientifically astounding

85
00:05:36,500 --> 00:05:42,100
is the causal chain: a strong youthful disillusionment triggered by a trauma, which silently

86
00:05:42,100 --> 00:05:47,399
settles over time and then reactivates almost 30 years later with objective support

87
00:05:47,399 --> 00:05:49,899
for movements that challenge the status quo.

88
00:05:49,899 --> 00:05:53,500
Of course, one might ask if there are other causes.

89
00:05:53,500 --> 00:05:57,500
The researchers asked themselves this and conducted extremely rigorous tests.

90
00:05:57,500 --> 00:06:02,000
Those same years were also marked by a brutal economic crisis and tragic

91
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:08,000
and bloody mafia attacks. Yet, by cross-referencing regional data, it became clear

92
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:12,779
that neither the collapse of the local economy nor the geographical proximity to those massacres

93
00:06:12,779 --> 00:06:16,500
produced a long-term political shift of that magnitude.

94
00:06:16,500 --> 00:06:21,620
The primary, unique, and dominant cause of this generational scar undoubtedly remains

95
00:06:21,620 --> 00:06:23,500
the corruption scandal.

96
00:06:23,500 --> 00:06:28,500
All of this leads us to a conclusion that is a true intellectual challenge.

97
00:06:28,500 --> 00:06:33,699
If it is scientifically proven that a single enormous historical trauma linked to corruption

98
00:06:33,699 --> 00:06:37,800
is capable of literally rewriting the political DNA of an entire generation for

99
00:06:37,800 --> 00:06:43,199
the subsequent 30 years, how will today's seismic crises shape tomorrow's voters?

100
00:06:43,199 --> 00:06:44,199
of tomorrow?

101
00:06:44,199 --> 00:06:48,699
It is evident that the institutional shocks of the present will have a very long echo.

102
00:06:48,699 --> 00:06:53,699
Today's analysis concludes here, leaving us with precisely this crucial question.